.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-admin-catalog-reload - Rebuild the catalogue snapshot from the database, in the process that answers (write mode, audited)


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 admin catalog reload [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Rebuild the chain catalogue's in-memory snapshot from the database. What the database
holds becomes what the process serves: a chain switched on starts taking payments, a
token retired stops being offered.

.PP
The command reads the diff first and asks, saying how many changes are pending. The
answer says what the rebuilt snapshot holds and what the reload applied.

.PP
ONE PROCESS, the one that answers the request. It is the tool for an environment served
by a single process. Where several serve (web machines, a worker) each has its own
snapshot and this reaches one of them: a restart is what reaches them all.

.PP
A write: it needs write mode ('rail0 admin write-mode on').


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for reload

.PP
\fB-y\fP, \fB--yes\fP[=false]
	Skip the confirmation prompt (required to run non-interactively)


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--agent\fP[=false]
	Agent mode: one JSON envelope on stdout, JSON events on stderr, no prompts (env: RAIL0_AGENT=1)

.PP
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 admin catalog reload
  rail0 admin catalog reload --yes
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-admin-catalog(1)\fP
